# Web Performance Rules

## Core Web Vitals Targets

| Metric | Target |
|--------|--------|
| LCP | < 2.5s |
| INP | < 200ms |
| CLS | < 0.1 |
| FCP | < 1.5s |
| TBT | < 200ms |

## Loading Strategy

1. Keep the initial Vite route shell small.
2. Use route- and feature-level dynamic imports for heavy surfaces (grid,
   charts, export, detail).
3. Wrap lazy modules in Suspense loading UI and an error boundary so a slow
   or failed chunk does not blank the shell.
4. Preload a chunk only on clear user intent (hover or focus of the next
   route). Do not prefetch every lazy module.
5. Import grid and chart code from concrete entry points. Avoid barrel files
   that pull TanStack Table, Virtual, Recharts, or unused shadcn modules into
   the initial chunk.

## Production Bundles

- Inspect the production graph for initial and async chunk sizes and
  duplicated dependencies. Set budgets from that evidence.
- Serve built chunks with content hashes and immutable cache headers.
- Avoid a broad vendor chunk. Add manual grouping only when measurements
  justify it.
- Preserve Vite's automatic splitting defaults. Do not add bundler-specific
  tuning until a measured regression requires it.
- Validate lazy chunks on a slow network and when a chunk fails to load.

## Image Optimization

- Explicit `width` and `height`
- `loading="eager"` plus `fetchpriority="high"` for hero media only
- `loading="lazy"` for below-the-fold assets
- Prefer AVIF or WebP with fallbacks
- Never ship source images far beyond rendered size

## Font Loading

- Max two font families unless there is a clear exception
- `font-display: swap`
- Subset where possible
- Preload only the truly critical weight/style

## Animation Performance

- Animate compositor-friendly properties only
- Use `will-change` narrowly and remove it when done
- Prefer CSS for simple transitions
- Use `requestAnimationFrame` or established animation libraries for JS motion
- Avoid scroll handler churn; use IntersectionObserver or well-behaved libraries

## Performance Checklist

- [ ] React remains responsive while query and export work runs
- [ ] GraphQL requests and Apollo partitions are bounded and free of waterfalls
- [ ] Export polling and downloads do not duplicate work
- [ ] Heavy routes and features load through dynamic imports with loading and
      error fallbacks
- [ ] Production chunks meet evidence-based size budgets and are not duplicated
      across the graph
- [ ] Built chunks are content-hashed and cached as immutable
- [ ] Lazy chunks remain usable on a slow network and after a load failure
- [ ] Grid row identity and rendering are stable
- [ ] Charts avoid unnecessary full-data copies and redraws
- [ ] No layout shifts from loading, error, or detail states
- [ ] Motion respects reduced-motion preferences
